      "Published October 13, 2026. Route Suspected Malicious Ticket Attachments starts when suspected malicious attachments affects a customer or operating queue. The immediate goal is not to guess a cause. It is to preserve the reported state, prevent an irreversible shortcut, and move a decision-ready record to the security incident owner. Open case OHS13-B12 with local time, impact, safe state, and promised update.",
      "## Define the support record\n\nFor suspected malicious attachments, checkpoint OHS13-B12-1 records the authoritative source, system state, observed symptom, instruction version, actor, timestamp and zone, customer-visible effect, current owner, due point, correction history, and destination receipt. During define the support record, test an ordinary case, missing evidence, conflicting signals, unavailable owner, denied action, system interruption, downstream rejection, and correction after apparent completion. Frontline staff may gather facts, apply approved routine steps, and draft bounded updates; the security incident owner retains security, privacy, identity, access, money, policy, and risk decisions. A pass requires source-linked evidence and attributable acceptance. A failure remains open with a stable reason and safe holding state; assignment, silence, or a closed label does not prove the outcome. Preserve contradictions instead of selecting a convenient explanation, and never request credentials, tokens, or restricted data to make the ticket look complete.",
